    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi i'm studying phonetics and i'm a bit confused about alveolar fricatives. can u explain the difference between the voiced /z/ and the unvoiced /s/ sounds? specifically how the vocal cords work for each one?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The main difference between the unvoiced /s/ and the voiced /z/ lies in vocal cord vibration. For /s/, your vocal cords remain open and loose, allowing air to pass through without vibration, producing a hissing sound. Conversely, for the voiced /z/, the vocal cords are brought together and vibrate steadily as the air passes over them, creating a buzzing sound. Think of it as a difference between blowing air versus buzzing with your throat.",
      "source": "target",
