    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so for the /s/ sound, it's basically just air moving through the teeth? is there a specific way the physical sensation in the throat changes when i switch from /s/ to /z/?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, for /s/, the constriction is formed mainly by the tongue near the alveolar ridge, creating turbulence in the airflow, but there is no vibration. When you move to /z/, the exact same tongue position is used, but the key change is the activation of the vocal folds causing them to buzz. You will physically feel a distinct vibration in your throat and mouth when producing /z/, which is absent when making /s/.",
